      "side": 1,
      "message": "Good question! I added this because I wanted to measure the overhead of starting/stopping the TRNG.\n\nMore context: the code in Google3 does not (currently) initialize/destroy the RNG. So if we want to use the TRNG without refactoring the Google3 code, one solution would be to have turn it on and off at any call.\n\nIt\u0027s not optimal in terms of performance, but it would work out of the box.\n\nBased on the results measured so far, I am leaning more towards using `std::rand()` though.",
